WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.433
- Thank you to Ancestry
for supporting PBS.

00:00:04.536 --> 00:00:06.520
(gasps) Philip, come here, look.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:07.353
- [Philip] What?

00:00:07.353 --> 00:00:09.400
- A huge tree fell on our
neighbor's car last night

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.610
in a storm.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:11.820
- Oh, that's awful.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.490
- It's amazing, we're going to Cabo, baby!

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:17.010
- What are you talking about?

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.160
- Well, I had a hunch

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.220
that something horrible
was gonna happen to them,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.110
so I went and bought

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.780
a massive insurance policy on their car.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.850
Make it rain, baby!

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.100
- Is that legal?

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.850
- Oh, you're such a Boy Scout.

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:30.980
Now, if I can get something bad

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.440
to happen to their cat,

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.516
we can upgrade to first class.

00:00:34.516 --> 00:00:35.846
(cat meows)

00:00:35.846 --> 00:00:37.427
(light upbeat music)

00:00:37.427 --> 00:00:38.640
(pen scrapes)

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.640
(coins tinkling)

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.688
(coins whoosh)

00:00:43.688 --> 00:00:44.521
(air whooshes)

00:00:44.521 --> 00:00:46.220
- Whenever you purchase an investment,

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.890
you're hoping that it will go up in value.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.890
By buying a company stock,
you win if they win.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.010
We're all in this together, right?

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.530
Well, not always.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.900
- Since the very beginning
of stock investing,

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.560
people have found a way to profit

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.510
when companies struggle or fail.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.990
The first instance was 1609,

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:07.810
when a Dutch businessman
named Isaac Le Maire

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.870
speculated that the Dutch
East India Company stock

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.780
was due for a big drop,

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.720
and he wanted to make some
money if he was right.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.140
In Amsterdam at the time,

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:18.920
there was already a system in place

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.730
to make money if prices of grain fell,

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.520
and that gave him an idea.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.670
Le Maire headed to the bridge in town

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:26.870
where stocks were traded

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:30.850
by an elaborate hand-slapping
system, no joke,

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.750
and found somebody to take his bet.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.420
- The process of speculating
on the future fall

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.570
of a company's stock is
called short selling,

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.700
or shorting, for short.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.210
To most people, the process
can seem confusing and complex

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:46.430
and it can create downward pressure

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.000
on a company or asset,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.510
potentially ensuring its doom.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.810
It's for these reasons that society tends

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.810
to dislike and distrust short sellers.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:59.220
In 1772, a short-selling
scheme precipitated

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:03.190
a near total collapse of
every bank in Scotland.

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:05.360
- Many Americans blame short sellers

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.130
for the Great Crash of 1929

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:08.750
that led to the Great Depression.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.880
And as recently as this year,

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.310
the short selling of
GameStop and AMC stock

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.170
created such a fervor

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.870
that it caught the attention
of Treasury Secretary Yellen

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.930
as a possible threat to
the stability of markets.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:23.650
- But how does this even work?

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:26.270
How do you bet that a stock will fall?

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:27.610
Essentially, the way to do it

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:30.340
is to borrow the stock
from a brokerage firm

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.050
and then immediately
sell it to someone else.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.230
This is where the term short came from.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:37.040
You are selling something you don't have,

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.690
or are short of.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.530
When the price of the
stock falls, as you expect,

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.630
you buy it back at the lower cost

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.170
and return it to the brokerage firm,

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.450
keeping the difference as profit.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:48.650
- Sounds simple enough,

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:50.490
but is this the kind of investment

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.450
that the average person should attempt?

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:54.440
- To really understand the promise

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.330
and perils of short selling,

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:57.720
I think we need to-

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.257
- [Both] Run the numbers.

00:02:59.257 --> 00:03:03.989
(dynamic classic game show music)

00:03:03.989 --> 00:03:06.290
- Jannik works in the floral industry,

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:10.200
and recently observed a
frenzy for tulip bulbs.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.020
It's gotten downright crazy lately

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:13.720
with people trading wedding bands

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.710
and even used cars for a single bulb.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:19.380
But Jannik knows a
bubble when he sees one,

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.650
so he hatches a plan
to short sell the stock

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:24.950
of the biggest tulip company in the world,

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.900
Tulips International, which
just hit an all-time high

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.130
of $200 per share.

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:32.670
- His first step is to
choose a brokerage firm

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.720
and open a special type of account

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.060
known as a margin account.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.840
This will allow him to
borrow shares directly

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.370
from the brokerage company.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:45.240
He wants to short sell 10
shares of Tulips International,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.090
so he borrows them from the brokerage

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:49.950
at the current price of $200 per share.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:51.510
In order to lend him these shares,

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.270
his brokerage wants him to
put some skin in the game

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:58.160
and requires him to deposit
25% of the loan amount

00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.820
in his margin account.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.300
That $500 is his only
personal capital contribution

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.270
at this point.

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:07.980
The firm loans Jannik
$2,000 worth of TLI stock

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.810
at 1% margin interest,

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.330
and he instantly turns around

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:13.960
and sells all 10 shares to Mila.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:17.070
He puts Mila's $2,000
into his margin account,

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:20.760
and now sits back to watch
the tulip bubble burst.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.070
- Let's just say that
Jannik's spidey sense

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.600
is proven right.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.850
Over the next 30 days,
the stock drops and drops,

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.220
losing half of its value.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:34.840
He buys the 10 stocks back from poor Mina

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:37.050
for just $100 a share,

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.410
and returns them to the brokerage firm,

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.270
pocketing the difference.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:44.580
Taking out $1.66 in interest for the loan,

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.760
and $10 in trading fees,

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:50.763
Jannik posted a gain of $988.34.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:55.220
That's 197% profit

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:58.530
on his original contribution of just $500.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.280
Sounds like a good gamble.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.170
But the real risk in short selling

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:04.810
isn't the upfront cost.

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:07.280
- What if Jannik turned out to be wrong?

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:08.880
What if the share price didn't fall

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.550
but continued its climb,

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:13.920
eventually reaching $300 per share?

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.340
This is bad news,

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:19.030
because remember, he doesn't
owe the brokerage $2,000.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:21.860
He owes them 10 shares
of Tulips International,

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:23.060
and he'll have to get them back

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.050
no matter how much it costs him.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:26.920
Maybe he thinks the price will still fall

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:28.150
and he can wait it out,

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:29.250
but the brokerage firm

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:32.600
won't allow his debt-to-deposit
ratio to get too high,

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:34.560
so he'll have to keep depositing money

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:35.750
into his margin account

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:37.950
as the price of the stock gets higher.

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:41.330
At this point, Jannik
admits he is no Nostradamus.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:42.940
He decides to cut his losses,

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:45.610
buy back the 10 shares for $3,000,

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:47.870
and return them to the brokerage company.

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:52.290
- This saddles him with
a realized loss of $1,000

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:53.920
on the sale alone.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.030
And once you include the trading fees

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:57.380
and interest he had to pay,

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:01.530
he's looking at a 202% loss,

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:06.130
which is obviously more than
his starting amount of $500.

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:09.610
- This is why short selling
is big-time risky business.

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:11.200
Typically, when you invest,

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.130
you can only lose up to the value

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:14.620
of the amount you purchased.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:18.150
100% loss is the worst-case scenario.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:19.170
But with shorting,

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.710
you can lose more than
your starting amount,

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:23.510
as poor Jannik found out.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:26.190
In fact, since the gains on the investment

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:28.080
are technically unlimited,

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:32.390
short selling creates the
possibility for infinite losses.

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:33.580
Yikes.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:36.810
- Nevertheless, short selling
is and always has been

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:39.690
a standard function of investment markets.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.930
Everyday investors like you and me,

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:44.010
all the way to Wall Street hedge funds,

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:46.350
can short nearly any type of investment,

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.440
hoping that the price drops.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:52.670
But sometimes investors
aren't content to just hope.

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:55.390
They want to make it drop.

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:57.740
- Remember our friend Isaac Le Maire?

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:00.910
After he shorted the world's
first stock in the 1600s,

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:02.150
he went around town

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:05.257
spreading rumors and
lies about the company.

00:07:05.257 --> 00:07:07.800
"Did you hear about the
Dutch East India ship

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:09.560
that sank off the coast,
(man gasps)

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.080
or how leaky seawater

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:12.752
is going to ruin the taste of pepper?"

00:07:12.752 --> 00:07:13.585
(woman gags)

00:07:13.585 --> 00:07:15.960
This would be considered unethical today.

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:17.200
But in a modern version,

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.480
when hedge funds team up
and heavily short a company,

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:22.680
it can shake investor confidence.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:23.810
The company goes into

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.740
an artificially accelerated death spiral,

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:30.160
with shorted investors
gleefully hovering like vultures

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:32.780
ready to swoop in and claim victory.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:35.430
- Well, at least that's
how it usually works.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:38.740
Sometimes the little guys
decide to fight back.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:42.550
Here to explain is CFA
investment analyst Richard Coffin

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:44.110
from The Plain Bagel.

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:45.920
- In January of 2021,

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:48.060
a group of investors online discovered

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:51.270
that GameStop, a struggling
video game retailer,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:54.670
was being excessively
short sold by hedge funds.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:56.540
But while a sizable short interest

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:58.600
is usually a bad omen,

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:01.080
these investors used this information

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.540
to send the stock skyrocketing

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:06.750
by triggering what's
known as a short squeeze.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:09.520
A short squeeze occurs
when a rising stock price

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:12.740
prompts a rush for the
exit by short sellers.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.000
Because these investors are losing money,

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.310
some may decide, or indeed be forced,

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:21.070
to cut their losses and buy back the stock

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:22.730
to close their position.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.350
But by abandoning the short sale ship,

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.570
these investors end up sinking it faster

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:29.450
for those left behind.

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:32.720
Their buy orders can push
the price higher still,

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:34.870
prompting other investors to jump ship

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:37.310
and buy the stock back themselves.

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:39.040
This positive feedback loop

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:42.750
can send a stock surging over
a very short period of time.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.570
And the larger the short
interest to begin with,

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:49.170
the more returns can be squeezed
out of the short sellers.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:50.510
After buying GameStop shares

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:52.280
to get the ball rolling themselves,

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:55.810
our online investors prompted
quite the surge in the stock,

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:59.770
with GameStop's share
price jumping over 1,700%

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:01.730
in January alone.

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:04.360
Needless to say, it was
quite the rude awakening

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:06.520
for those who thought GameStop's decline

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:08.280
was a sure bet.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.230
- The long and the short of it is,

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:12.410
yes, you can make real money

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:14.360
when an investment falls in value.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:15.370
But it's speculative,

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:17.420
and exposes you to the possibility

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:19.140
of unlimited losses.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:20.940
- So before you dip your toe

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:22.790
into the world of short selling,

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:25.140
make sure you understand the risks,

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.410
are highly familiar with investing tools,

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:29.000
and aren't gambling with money

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:31.870
you can't afford to lose and then some.

00:09:31.870 --> 00:09:33.550
- [Both] And that's our two cents.

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:36.600
